# {% project_name %}

A [Soroban](https://developers.stellar.org/docs/build/smart-contracts) smart
contract for the Stellar network, generated by
[Lumenprint](https://github.com/LumenAnvil/lumenprint).

This template is about **events**: how a contract tells the outside world that
something happened. It keeps a running counter in instance storage and emits an
event on every change, so you can watch state and events move together.

## What it does

| Function | Emits | Purpose |
| -------- | ----- | ------- |
| `ping(who)` | topics `(ping, who)`, data `count` | A signed ping; shows a filterable event tied to an address |
| `announce(message)` | topic `(announce,)`, data `message` | A pure broadcast; shows an event with no storage behind it |
| `count()` | — | Reads the counter without emitting anything |

## Events, in one minute

Events are declared as types with the `#[contractevent]` macro. Each has two
parts:

- **topics** — the fields marked `#[topic]`, led by the event name (the struct
  name in snake case). Indexers filter on these, so keep them small.
- **data** — the remaining fields: the payload.

```rust
#[contractevent]
pub struct Ping {
    #[topic]
    pub who: Address,   // a topic, alongside the "ping" name
    pub count: u32,     // the data
}

// Emit it:
Ping { who, count }.publish(&env);
```

This replaces the older, now-deprecated `env.events().publish(topics, data)`.
Declaring events as types is type-safe and records each event in the contract's
interface spec, so tooling can decode them.

Events are **not** storage. A contract cannot read its own past events, and they
need no time-to-live upkeep. They exist for off-chain indexers, front-ends and
notifications. The tests in `src/test.rs` read them back with the SDK's `Events`
test interface and an event type's `to_xdr` helper — the standard way to test
that a contract emits what it should.

You can inspect the event shapes a deployed build declares with
[Lumenscan](https://github.com/LumenAnvil/lumenscan).

